<Project>

  <!--
    Every packable project carries a committed baseline of its public API.

    A published version number is a promise: the surface it shipped stays available for as long as
    the major version does. Nothing enforced that promise here — PublicApiContractTests checks what
    the API *does* (the list it hands out cannot be written through, the contracts it refuses), not
    what it *is*, so a removed overload or a narrowed return type would ship silently under a
    version claiming compatibility.

    Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers closes that: every public symbol must appear in
    PublicAPI.Shipped.txt or PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt beside the project, so a change to the surface
    is a reviewed diff in a text file rather than an accident inside a commit about something else.
    An undeclared public symbol raises RS0016; a declared symbol that no longer exists raises
    RS0017. Both are warnings by default and errors here, because TreatWarningsAsErrors is on for
    the whole repository — which is the point: the build refuses the change until the baseline
    records it.

    Wired here rather than in each project so the rule reads as what it is — a property of being
    packable, not a chore two csproj files happen to remember. Directory.Build.targets is imported
    after the project body, which is why $(IsPackable) can be tested at all: in Directory.Build.props
    it is not yet set.

    Accepting an intended surface change: apply the "Add to public API" fix on RS0016 in an IDE, or
    run `dotnet format analyzers` on the project, restricted to the RS0016 diagnostic, to append the
    entries; a removal is a line deleted by hand. New entries land in PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt and
    move to PublicAPI.Shipped.txt when the version that ships them is released.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' == 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" Version="5.6.0" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
